{"instructions": "Explain how a university registration system should handle two students simultaneously requesting the last seat. Discuss transactions, duplicate prevention, and the benefits and risks of caching. This is a theoretical answer, not a coding task.", "criterion": "Correct atomicity and rollback", "rubric": {"policy": "Assess theoretical understanding. A valid conceptual explanation suffices; do not require SQL code, every listed alternative, or topics outside the selected criterion. Separate independent criteria. An explicitly false claim is incorrect, not just missing coverage.", "criterion": "Correct atomicity and rollback", "professional_principle": "Atomicity groups the enrollment write and seat-count update as one all-or-nothing unit. If either fails, rollback undoes all changes in that transaction. Atomicity is distinct from isolation and capacity-race protection.", "valid_solutions": ["A transaction encompassing both changes, committing together on success and rolling back both on any failure.", "An equivalent conceptual all-or-nothing operation with explicit failure reversal."], "scope": "Credit explicit transaction grouping plus rollback without requiring the word atomicity or an isolation-level name. Evaluate cache safety and concurrent capacity reservation in their own criteria; neither substitutes for nor negates an otherwise correct description of rollback."}, "submission": "I would add a UNIQUE constraint on student ID and course ID. This guarantees that two different students cannot take the last seat. A transaction can update the seat count and save the enrollment together, with rollback if either fails. A cache makes reads faster. If the cache says a seat is available, we must check the database before confirming enrollment because cached availability may be stale."}
